Question 2) According to VirusTotal, what is the full filetype of the provided sample? (Format: X X X X)
Moving to the VirusTotal website, I loaded that file into the website. Conveniently in the Details pane, a few lines down, we see a File Type line with the value: Office Open XML Document.

Question 3) Extract the URL that is used within the sample and submit it (Format: https://x.domain.tld/path/to/something)
If we then make our way to the Relations tab, we see "Contacted URLs." The longest/most specific one is  	https://www.xmlformats.com/office/word/2022/wordprocessingDrawing/RDF842l.html, so I tried that as the answer and it works!

Question 4) What is the name of the XML file that is storing the extracted URL? (Format: file.name.ext)
I went to "Bundled Files," and the first one is:
word/_rels/document.xml.rels

Question 5) The extracted URL accesses a HTML file that triggers the vulnerability to execute a malicious payload. According to the HTML processing functions, any files with fewer than <Number> bytes would not invoke the payload. Submit the <Number> (Format: Number of Bytes)
Now I have to resort to Google for this one.
This article here gives us some good info about the Follina vulnerability itself:
https://www.hackthebox.com/blog/cve-2022-30190-follina-explained
And mentions that "The secondary payload must surpass 4096 bytes to evade specific size checks."

Question 6) After execution, the sample will try to kill a process if it is already running. What is the name of this process? (Format: filename.ext)
Going to another site (which also would have answered the previous question, but I digress).
https://www.huntress.com/blog/microsoft-office-remote-code-execution-follina-msdt-bug
It tells us that there is a Base64 string that decodes to:
taskkill /f /im msdt.exe
Answer: msdt.exe

Question 7) You were asked to write a process-based detection rule using Windows Event ID 4688. What would be the ProcessName and ParentProcessname used in this detection rule? [Hint: OSINT time!] (Format: ProcessName, ParentProcessName)
The above link also gives us this information:
"Payloads executed by this attack vector will create a child process of msdt.exe under the offending Microsoft Office parent."
So the answer is "msdt.exe, winword.exe."

Question 8) Submit the MITRE technique ID used by the sample for Execution [Hint: Online sandbox platforms can help!] (Format: TXXXX)
Go back to VirusTotal, under Behavior, and I can also see the MITRE techniques for each aspect of the malware. I just tried each one under Execution, and the answer was T1559.

Question 9) Submit the CVE associated with the vulnerability that is being exploited (Format: CVE-XXXX-XXXXX)
Simple Google search, first hit from OWASP, gives us this answer: CVE-2022-30190.
